# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored; never commit real values.
# On Render these same names go in the dashboard's Environment tab instead.

# Signs the session cookie. The app refuses to boot without it.
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_hex(32))"
FLASK_SECRET_KEY=

OPENAI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# ---- Google sign-in ----------------------------------------------------
# Both must be set or the "Continue with Google" button stays disabled and
# renders a "setup needed" badge (auth.py's _auth_context).
#
# Create them at https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials:
#   Create credentials -> OAuth client ID -> Web application
#   Authorized redirect URIs -- add BOTH, exactly, no trailing slash:
#     http://localhost:5000/auth/google/callback
#     https://repcheck-q0m4.onrender.com/auth/google/callback
#   (add one per port if you run worktrees on 5050 etc.)
#
# The redirect URI Google receives is built from the incoming request, so
# it must match one of the entries above character for character or Google
# refuses with redirect_uri_mismatch before the user ever comes back here.
#
# The OAuth consent screen also needs the email/profile/openid scopes and,
# while it is in "Testing" mode, every account that will sign in listed
# under Test users -- otherwise Google shows "access blocked" to everyone
# but the owner. Publishing the app removes that limit.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Sign in with Apple (optional -- the button shows a "setup needed" badge
# and goes nowhere until all four of these are set).
#
# From https://developer.apple.com/account/resources/identifiers, with a
# paid Apple Developer account:
#   1. Register an App ID with the "Sign in with Apple" capability.
#   2. Register a Services ID (e.g. com.repcheck.web) -- that identifier,
#      NOT the App ID or the iOS bundle ID, is APPLE_CLIENT_ID.
#   3. Configure the Services ID: domain repcheck-q0m4.onrender.com, and
#      Return URL https://repcheck-q0m4.onrender.com/auth/apple/callback
#      (add one per environment; Apple compares it byte-for-byte and
#      refuses http:// and bare localhost, so Apple sign-in cannot be
#      exercised against the plain-HTTP local dev server).
#   4. Keys -> new key with Sign in with Apple enabled. The download is a
#      one-time .p8 file: its contents are APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY and the key
#      identifier is APPLE_KEY_ID. APPLE_TEAM_ID is the 10-character team
#      id in the top right of the developer portal.
#
# APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY is a multi-line PEM and this file is single-line, so
# paste the whole .p8 on one line -- header and footer lines included, every
# newline written as a literal backslash-n, the whole thing in quotes:
#   APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY="<BEGIN line>\n<base64 body>\n<END line>"
APPLE_CLIENT_ID=
APPLE_TEAM_ID=
APPLE_KEY_ID=
APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY=

